<p>The US Supreme Court ruled 6–3 that President Trump exceeded his authority when he used the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) to impose broad “reciprocal” tariffs on imports from many US trading partners. The decision strikes down most of that tariff architecture and adds new uncertainty to global trade and ongoing deal-making. Chief Justice John Roberts wrote the opinion, joined by a cross-ideological majority that included both conservative and liberal justices. Within hours, Trump announced a new temporary 10% “global tariff” under Section 122 of the Trade Act of 1974, pitching it as a different legal path after the ruling. He also signaled more moves through new investigations into allegedly unfair trade practices under Section 301, while insisting key negotiations and agreements would continue as governments and businesses reassess what comes next. For the rest of the world, the immediate bright spot is a clearer judicial limit on emergency economic powers—and, at least in the near term, the prospect of lower baseline tariffs than some partners had faced. Still, the next phase looks likely to shift toward slower, more process-driven trade tools.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/AG48V8c0rGdrCPK3oAivp5WxCJ-A_HZv3MYkBU8nc5tM4OyJcCHWjOL-YMHut0iOUkZawyNINl5eIk5Y_JPKJ90pIBgShvpH0-E16eGTaT2QiJKi4Li5Jc3g8FK-V-s6W1ompx1AaVUAdUlOjgHQ14AkkeAxNW_pNEHRDEe7yQ4Wj3coFhNl74lGZyI4Y0qkGjF9jduRNQehQZJbPwTptwZvBOkzsgApcnoUEuY' alt='President Trump at the White House during an earlier tariff announcement.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Refund questions: The court did not say what should happen to more than $130 billion already collected under the struck-down tariffs, fueling political pressure for refunds.</li><li>Section 122’s rationale: Section 122 comes with a 15%/150-day ceiling and is framed around “large and serious” balance-of-payments problems. Unlike Section 301, it does not require a lengthy investigative process—raising questions about how the administration will justify a blanket surcharge in practice.</li><li>China trip set: The White House confirmed Trump will travel to China from March 31 to April 2 for talks with President Xi Jinping. The visit is expected to test whether the existing US-China trade truce can be extended after the court ruling.</li><li>Market snap reaction: Dawn reported that stocks rose and Treasury yields climbed after the verdict, a sign investors are repricing legal risk around the administration’s tariff toolkit even as policy uncertainty persists.</li></ul><blockquote>Today, I will sign an order to impose a 10 percent global tariff under Section 122, over and above our normal tariffs already being charged. - Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: He called the ruling “deeply disappointing,” criticized the justices who sided against him, and argued he can pursue tariffs through other statutes while keeping some existing Section 232 and Section 301 tariffs in place. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/02/20/donald-trump-tariff-supreme-court-reaction-00791245'>Politico</a>)</li><li>US Supreme Court majority: Chief Justice John Roberts wrote that IEEPA’s power to “regulate… importation” does not include the power to impose tariffs, reinforcing Congress’s constitutional role over taxation and duties. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/20/2026/supreme-court-crushes-trumps-tariff-regime'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Justice Brett Kavanaugh (dissent): In dissent, he pointed to other countries’ use of trade measures and referenced US duties tied to India’s Russian oil purchases as part of his argument for broader presidential latitude. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/judge-refers-to-indias-russian-oil-duties-in-dissent-as-us-supreme-court-strikes-down-trump-s-tariffs-13982124.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li><li>US Democrats in Congress: Some Democrats, including Sen. Elizabeth Warren, said the tariff regime caused economic harm and renewed calls for stronger congressional checks on the administration’s trade powers after the ruling. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/eu-democrats-world-leaders-react-us-supreme-court-ruling-trump-sweeping-tariffs-13982117.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li><li>Canada: Canada welcomed the decision, calling the earlier levies unjustified, while saying it will watch closely for whatever replacement measures come next. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/democrats-demand-refunds-after-us-high-court-tosses-trump-tariffs'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>US Democratic governors: California Gov. Gavin Newsom and Illinois Gov. JB Pritzker demanded that tariff revenue be refunded, with Pritzker seeking nearly $9 billion for Illinois families. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/democrats-demand-refunds-after-us-high-court-tosses-trump-tariffs'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/trump-10-tariffs-us-supreme-court-ruling-refunds-trade-deals-13982164.html'>Why has Trump imposed 10% tariffs after US SC ruling? What happens next?</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/trump-ends-some-tariffs-imposes-new-10-global-one/a-76063714'>Trump ends some tariffs, imposes new 10% global one</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/feb/20/us-iran-nuclear-deal-trump-agreement-strikes-latest-news-live-updates'>Trump announces new 10% global tariffs, lashes out at supreme court justices for ‘ridiculous’ ruling - as it happened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/us-court-overturns-trump-global-tariffs-what-it-means-mena'>US court overturns Trump global tariffs: What it means for MENA</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/usa/2026-02-21/these-are-the-many-countries-that-stand-to-gain-from-the-supreme-court-ruling-overturning-trumps-tariffs.html'>These are the 